Chapter: Builtins
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The builtins, once. A builtin name used to appear in three hand-maintained
 lists that agreed only by inspection, and each way of disagreeing failed
 differently and at its own distance from the cause: absent from the
 resolver was CDX3002, which kills not just the call but every chapter
 downstream of the one that made it; routed with no emitter was CDX2042 at
 emit. A build-time guard reconciled them, which is not the same as removing
 the disagreement -- it only meant a disagreement could not ship.

 Two of the three are gone. The routing list is derived from the emitter
 table (see the X86-64 Code Generator), so being routed IS having an
 emitter. This table is the other two: the names the resolver admits, and
 the types the checker binds. `builtin-names` is the names in it;
 `builtin-type-env` is a fold of `env-bind` over the ones that carry a type.
 Neither is written out by hand any more, so neither can drift from this.

 Eight names carry no type, and each is deliberate. `True`, `False` and
 `Nothing` are special-cased by the checker -- that is how CDX2086 gets to
 say that Nothing is a type and not a value. `open-file`, `close-file`,
 `read-all`, `now` and `random-integer` are effect operations, typed by
 their `effect` declarations rather than here. A name with no type resolves
 and is not bound; that is what `None` means in this table, and it is a
 statement rather than an omission.

 The order is the order the let-chain bound in, and it is load-bearing:
 `env-bind` REPLACES, so where a name appears twice the LAST one wins.
 `process-exit` appears twice, with two different types, and the second is
 not the one every transpiler emitter implements -- so `process-exit 0` does
 not type-check. That is BACKLOG 2.29, and it is a decision rather than a
 typo: two real operations collided on one name. The rows are kept next to
 each other and in order, so the fold still resolves it exactly as the
 let-chain did. What has changed is that you can see it. A table that hid
 the collision is what let it live.

Section: The Table
  BuiltinSpec = record {
   bs-name : Text,
   bs-type : Maybe CodexType
  }

 The four session-channel type names were `let` bindings inside the chain,
 which a table cannot hold. They are definitions now, so the type
 expressions below are the ones that were there, unedited.

  ssend-name : Name = Name { value = "SSend" }
  srecv-name : Name = Name { value = "SRecv" }
  send-name : Name = Name { value = "SEnd" }
  schan-name : Name = Name { value = "SChan" }

